"Compares study or subgroup effect estimates with intervals and optionally a weighted pooled effect.\n\nUse when: Several independent estimates measure the same effect on a compatible scale. Readers need study uncertainty, relative weight, and a synthesis together.\n\nAvoid when: Estimates combine incompatible outcomes or a pooled result lacks a defensible statistical model.\n\nVisual grammar: Point position encodes effect; square area may encode study weight. Horizontal intervals encode uncertainty, and an optional diamond encodes pooled estimate and bounds.\n\nBuild: Choose an effect scale and a null reference appropriate to differences or ratios. Plot one aligned study row with its interval and weight. Add a pooled estimate only after specifying the synthesis model and heterogeneity assumptions.\n\nCheck: Check ratio measures use a suitable log scale with null at one. Verify interval levels and weighting methods are stated. Confirm displayed study weights sum to the intended total.\n\nPitfalls: A narrow pooled interval does not remove between-study heterogeneity. Reserve separate columns for text and values so intervals never cross study labels.\n\nExample: Summarize synthetic material tests with standardized effects, 95% intervals, weights, and an explicitly modeled pooled estimate.\n\nAlternatives: Error bar chart, Dot plot, Gradient interval plot."
